# use-review.nuon — who read each `use` against its `source` PRD and its live
# route, and what they read.
#
# Spec: `prds/06-help/01-content-model/prd.md` R2's `use` sub-box and R5's
# clause "`use` describes the real gesture, never restates the key" — the
# gesture half, "the actual gesture, in order, including what to press next and
# what comes back".
#
# Why a record and not a check. It is the same reason `why-review.nuon` exists,
# and the same two measurements stand behind it: whole-text content-word
# containment (corpus mean 0.11) and best-sentence containment (mean 0.157)
# both put the known defects at or below the corpus mean. A third lexical proxy
# is not to be built. Whether a `use` describes the gesture the specification
# and the configuration actually perform is a reading, and a reading is what
# this file records.
#
# **The premise that kept this clause open for three sessions was wrong.** The
# box said "80 of 84 entries have no deployed surface to be checked against".
# Measured 2026-08-21: every one of the 84 entries carries a `source:` naming a
# PRD, all 84 resolve (`tests/help-content-model.nu` already enforces that),
# and the `source` PRD is the specification the `use` is supposed to describe.
# `~/.config/nushell`, `~/.config/nvim` and `~/.config/wezterm` all exist, so
# 79 of the 84 have a live route to read as well. Only `capsule.nuon`'s five
# have neither, and every capsule row below says so in its `note`.
#
# The method is the one that found the `Ctrl-T` defect the node's history
# cites as proof the clause was ungated: read the binding, follow the call
# chain, say what actually comes back. `config.nu:635` -> `config.nu:686` ->
# `finder.nu:33`. It worked; what was missing was the obligation to run it.
#
# **The digest keys on `use` AND `source`, and that is the difference from
# `why-review.nuon`.** A row here says "this prose matches that spec" — a claim
# about a pair — so editing either side invalidates it. Re-pointing an entry at
# a different PRD without re-reading the `use` against it is drift that
# `why-review.nuon`'s shape structurally cannot catch.
#
# Coverage differs too: `why` is optional, so only `why`-carrying entries need
# a row there. `use` is REQUIRED, so **all 84 entries need a current row here**
# and an entry with none is a violation. This record is complete by
# construction rather than by whoever remembered.
#
# The ritual, three steps:
#
#   1. read the entry's `use` against its `source` PRD, and — unless the
#      surface genuinely does not exist — against the live route: the binding,
#      the function it sends, what that function does, what comes back. Fix
#      what is wrong. Where the `use` and the PRD disagree about the gesture,
#      the PRD wins and the `use` changes; a `use` is never "fixed" by editing
#      another node's PRD (laws.md 2). If the PRD looks wrong, that is a
#      corrections filing and the row says so.
#   2. run `nu tests/help-content-model.nu`. It prints the digest to set.
#   3. add or update the row: id, file, digest, reviewer (your session id),
#      date. If you also wrote or revised the `use`, record `author` — and
#      expect the gate to refuse the row, because a reader who is the writer is
#      the record vouching for its own writing. `note` is optional and carries
#      the reading, so a later reader can check the judgement rather than
#      trust it.
#
# `author` is optional for the same reason it is optional in
# `why-review.nuon`: omitting it is invisible in the data, so this hardens an
# honest record rather than defeating a careless one. It is not backfilled onto
# rows whose text predates the field.
#
# 2026-08-21 · the sweep that created this file, and the shape of its
# independence. Session `impl-H-1` dispatched four readers — `impl-H-1-r1`
# (shell, 41), `impl-H-1-r2` (nvim, 27), `impl-H-1-r3` (terminal, 11),
# `impl-H-1-r4` (capsule, 5) — each of which had written none of the text it
# read and was asked to refute rather than to bless. They returned 14 defects.
#
# `impl-H-1` rewrote those 14 and is therefore their `author`, so it could not
# review them. A fifth reader, `impl-H-1-r5`, re-read all 14 against spec and
# live route: it refuted one (`cd <path>` — the correction had over-corrected
# in the other direction) and found two more still missing a step (`help` had
# dropped the last clause of the PRD's resolution order; `zl <query>` described
# a substitution where the live route makes an addition). Those three were
# rewritten a second time, which made `impl-H-1-r5` their author's collaborator
# rather than their reader, so a sixth, `impl-H-1-r6`, read the second round.
# It vouched all three, with the caveats recorded in their notes.
#
# That is why the `reviewer` column is not uniform: 11 rows carry `-r5`, 3
# carry `-r6`, and the 70 that were never rewritten carry the reader that first
# read them. Every one of the 14 rewritten rows carries the finding in its
# `note`, with the `file:line` that was read, so the judgement can be checked
# rather than trusted.
#
# Rewriting a `use` also invalidates that entry's row in `why-review.nuon`,
# because that digest keys on the `use`/`why` pair. Nine entries were affected;
# `impl-H-1-r6` and `impl-H-1-r7` re-read them there. See that file's header.
#
# Findings this sweep produced that this file cannot fix, recorded so they are
# not lost — each belongs to another node:
#
#   - `shell.nuon [z <query>]`'s `why` says "a failed `z` leaves no trace", and
#     04-shell/03-zoxide's acceptance says the same. Live, a no-match
#     `__zoxide_z` yields an empty path, `cd ""` reaches `mkcd` which reads
#     empty as no-argument and goes HOME (`~/.zoxide.nu:40-43`,
#     `config.nu:275`), so PWD moves and HOME is logged to recents
#     (`config.nu:441`). Not on the live-bug table.
#   - `cc`/`cr` always show a login picker (`config.nu:213,231-239`) although
#     04-shell/08-claude-launchers R3 says a single profile launches directly.
#     Undeclared divergence; wants an L-row.
#   - `terminal.nuon [Ctrl+Shift+B]`'s verify target false-passes off the live
#     wallpaper prompt (`wezterm.lua:1094-1096`) exactly as `Ctrl+Shift+T`'s
#     does off `SpawnTab`. The file header records the problem for `T` only.
#   - `capabilities-terminal.md:208-209` claims copy mode keeps "all 55 builtin
#     motions and searches". The live table has 55 rows, one of them this
#     config's own `c`, and no search key at all.
#   - four `02-terminal` PRD requirements contradict the live config
#     (close-confirmation, copy mode on `F3`, `Ctrl+C` copying the visible
#     screen) and `Ctrl+V`/`Ctrl+C` have no requirement anywhere in that
#     subtree despite citing its epic as `source`.
#   - `01-capsule/01-container-lifecycle` R6 names two cleanup modes and
#     neither invocation, so `capsule clean`'s gesture cannot be written down
#     honestly until it is corrected.
#   - four `nvim-map` verify targets omit `desc` (`gd and gI`,
#     `<leader>rn and <leader>ca`) where live sets `LSP: …` strings — four
#     drift-check false failures waiting for `06-help/04-drift-check`.

[
    # shell.nuon
    {id: "z <query>", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "dd6a50adc53794d8", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r1", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "zi", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "46869234abdbc0ec", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r5", author: "impl-H-1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "rewritten this session: the old text said only `a picker opens` and never named it. Live route `_zi_nav` (config.nu:444-448) -> `__zoxide_zi` (~/.zoxide.nu:47-49) -> `zoxide query --interactive`, which is fzf. Decision D.1c (prds/00-delivery/decisions/fzf/prd.md:31-40) makes fzf an accepted exception AND requires help to say so; no entry did."}
    {id: "cdi", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "99ddb8576c553ed6", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "defers wholesale to `zi` — `alias cdi = zi` at config.nu:150 is a forward reference that resolves to the later `_zi_nav`. It inherits whatever `zi` says, so it stands once `zi` names fzf."}
    {id: "zz", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "00734bc1b5dda650", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r1", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "zl <query>", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "4b072327d3fa75ee", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r6", author: "impl-H-1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "rewritten this session, then read by a reader who wrote none of it. The defect was the old rationale (`for when you want the hidden files too`), which was false: the PWD hook already runs `la` after a move (config.nu:399-406, la at config.nu:142), so zl's own la (config.nu:465-469) is a second listing, not the only one. `no file-opening branch, unlike z` verified — zl calls __zoxide_z directly while z's file branch is in _z_nav (config.nu:437-443), matching 04-shell/03-zoxide R3. VOUCHED WITH TWO CAVEATS, recorded rather than silently fixed: (a) the double listing needs PWD to actually move, since the hook is guarded `$after != $before` and `is-terminal --stdout` (config.nu:399) — `zl` with no argument at $HOME lists once; the query branch cannot hit this because zoxide queries with --exclude $PWD (~/.zoxide.nu:39); (b) `after every interactive move` is not literally every move — the bare-word jump suppresses the hook through $env._NAV (config.nu:404)."}
    {id: "zc <query>", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "34e6f96cb2d5ae2e", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "`same query as z` is loose in the same way `zl`'s was — `zc` calls `__zoxide_z` directly (config.nu:456-459) and so has no file-opening branch. Left standing: the sentence points at the query, and correcting it would be a sweep rather than a finding."}
    {id: "<word>", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "f9f1c1e7736f2c71", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r1", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "cd <path>", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "91f0d589ffd2e417", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r6", author: "impl-H-1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "rewritten twice this session. Round 1 replaced `one key to confirm` (wrong: the reflex `y` cancels); round 2 fixed round 1's own over-correction, `any other key prints aborted`, which was wrong the other way. The test is `input --numchar 1` then `if ($ans | str trim) != \"\" { print \"aborted\"; return }` (config.nu:279-283), so the predicate is non-blank, not not-Enter: the reader re-derived the trim semantics in nu and confirmed Enter, Space and Tab all confirm while `y` cancels. The live prompt string itself says `All else will cancel` (config.nu:279), which is the misleading source the entry now deliberately overrides. CAVEAT recorded: `everything else about it is the builtin` glosses one side effect — a successful move also writes startdir.txt via _startdir_save (config.nu:287) — which the `why` and the `mkcd` entry carry, but which makes that sentence slightly too strong."}
    {id: "mkcd", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "7b0bfdafbe27d5ea", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "the funnel claim holds (alias cd = mkcd at config.nu:289, picker cd at finder.nu:171, fallback cd at config.nu:511). Qualifier: the bare-word fallback pushes the dirstack and logs recents itself (config.nu:515-516) because the hook may not fire from pre_execution, so `the PWD hook is the single place that reacts` is the epic's invariant rather than literal live behaviour."}
    {id: "ls", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "2029fcc64e9cebf7", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r1", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "ls -D", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "d8fc8eb65860526e", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "spec-correct against 04-shell/06-listing R3, but the live route is `^du -sb` (config.nu:93), which macOS du does not support and whose stderr is discarded, so sizes silently stay inode sizes. Live bug L-1, declared in the file header and in the entry's own `why`."}
    {id: "l / ll / la", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "db6d8a2225539ce8", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r1", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "Ctrl-Space / F1", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "228824a846135cfd", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "route walked config.nu:594,608 -> tv_remote (config.nu:706) -> _finder_pick_channel (finder.nu:67). Two qualifiers left standing: `Tab` multi-selects in the tv UI (finder.nu:39) but _finder_open acts on `$sel | first` only (finder.nu:161); and `a commit opens in git show` is spec (04-shell/04-television R3) but dead live — the Commits decoder reads field 1 of a line already reduced to a bare hash (finder.nu:138-145), live bug L-2."}
    {id: "Ctrl-T", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "1be5c217d5678169", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "the precedent entry for this whole record. Route re-walked: config.nu:635 finder_pick -> config.nu:686 tv_finder -> finder with no --start -> finder.nu:33 _finder_pick_channel, so the channel remote really does open first. The fix that this node's history records still holds."}
    {id: "Ctrl-Q", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "e6708cd7b6a97fd0", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "replay route confirmed at quicklist.nu:36-41,46-54. Qualifier: finder picks are NOT logged live — the only _recents_add callers are the two zoxide wrappers and the fallback (config.nu:436,441,447,516). Spec-correct per 04-shell/07-quicklist R2, header-declared, live bug L-4."}
    {id: "finder", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "b0bb34775ab02f8b", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "`--start` and the typed decode are exact (finder.nu:23-45,122-152). Qualifier: the promised {hash, subject} from git-log never materialises live — same L-2 decoder bug at finder.nu:138-145."}
    {id: "tv channel", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "bd6d30e1fc1207bf", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "correct in substance against 04-shell/04-television R5. Qualifier: `under ~/.config/television` is loose — a cable file has to go in the `cable/` subdirectory, and a TOML dropped beside config.toml registers nothing. Left standing because `under` is true of the tree."}
    {id: "grep", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "2285dc4d5a19c853", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r1", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "help --fuzzy", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "d1590c17cdd47595", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "no live route exists; read against 06-help/03-browser R2/R4/R5 (prd.md:26-37) alone."}
    {id: "Ctrl-R", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "6cc11fbcdfa66f57", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r1", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "Alt-R", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "0cf77abe56c2b841", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "route confirmed config.nu:557 -> tv's own tv_shell_history (~/.cache/television/init.nu:18-29) over the nu-history channel, which has no cwd filter (cable/nu-history.toml:14). Unstated nuance left standing: it is also prefilled with the line up to the cursor (init.nu:21-23), which the Ctrl-R entry does mention and this one does not."}
    {id: "Up / Down", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "a5114d1d39992fbc", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "the cycle and the typing-resets rule are exact (config.nu:345-360). Qualifier: the binding is an `until` chain that tries menuup/menudown first (config.nu:564-577), so with a completion menu open the arrows move the menu; the `use` only implies this and the `why` carries it."}
    {id: "Shift+Up / Shift+Down", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "4a4afce1730be02d", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r1", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "Esc", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "448254bde78b4690", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r5", author: "impl-H-1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "rewritten this session, `use` and `title` both. The old text promised a two-stage escape; the live binding is `name: esc_clear ... event: { edit: clear }` (config.nu:626-631) — one unconditional edit command, in a block where every menu-safe binding is explicitly written as an `until` chain (config.nu:564-577). The two-branch behaviour existed only in the comment above the binding (config.nu:625), and the source PRD does not spec Esc at all."}
    {id: "cat <file>", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "4d8b4022c342de87", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r1", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "nv / vi", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "9ab0ad543f110e5b", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r1", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "nn", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "529cb53be73a8823", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r1", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "cf <file>", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "c916f5f710f0912e", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r1", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "g", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "bed5c35716db75c1", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r1", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "lg", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "b0b1ce1f3cdf95a5", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r1", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "q / :q / /exit", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "c208a0414ac597de", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r1", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "cc [...args]", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "2ac0ecd8930a030c", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "spec-faithful: 04-shell/08-claude-launchers R3 says a single profile launches directly with no picker (prd.md:23-27). Live disagrees — `cc` -> _claude_run -> _claude_login always runs `input list --fuzzy \"claude login\"` over [default, new login] (config.nu:213,231-239) — and this divergence is on NEITHER the file header's declared list nor the live-bug table. Left standing against the PRD, which wins; it wants an L-row."}
    {id: "cr [...args]", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "fa78ccd0ded3d004", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "`def cr` prepends --resume (config.nu:242), matching 04-shell/08 R2. Same undeclared login-picker divergence as `cc [...args]`."}
    {id: "help --json", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "2f62eeee2bcb510a", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "no live route; read against 06-help/05-agent-interface R1/R3 (prd.md:21-32) alone."}
    {id: "help --md", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "7e9793eeef69f646", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "no live route; read against 06-help/05-agent-interface R2 (prd.md:26-28) alone."}
    {id: "idioms", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "1acb724adbd09dab", reviewer: "reader-D-1c-spec03", author: "impl-D-1c-spec03", date: "2026-08-21", note: "carried forward from impl-H-1-r1 and re-verified: the four rules are 06-help/05-agent-interface R5 (prd.md:43-47) — rg/fd over grep/find, tv as the picker, `| where` not `| grep`, `cd` creating its target — and the entry still states exactly four. r1's qualifier is now RESOLVED rather than open: it flagged `Pick with television (tv), not fzf` as the very clause decision D.1c qualified, and this edit names the exception in place of asserting a rule the environment breaks; R4's bullet (prd.md:35-36) that an agent should read `television (tv)` and stop reaching for fzf still holds, because naming the one place fzf belongs is what stops it being reached for elsewhere. Checked the rewritten clause against the live route, not the board: config.nu:150 `alias cdi = zi`, config.nu:444 `_zi_nav` -> `__zoxide_zi` (~/.zoxide.nu:47-49) -> `zoxide query --interactive`; zoxide 0.9.9 carries the fzf spawn path in its binary (`could not find fzf, is it installed?`, `_ZO_FZF_OPTS`, `--height=45%`) and fzf is present at /opt/homebrew/bin/fzf — so `zi ... opens fzf` is true. `the only place fzf belongs` holds too: every other fzf mention under ~/.config/nushell is a comment — config.nu:683 calls tv's behaviour fzf-style, and overlay.nu:4 is the nu-native overlay, which is DO NOT PORT (04-shell/prd.md:65). Consistent with 04-shell I3 and 03-zoxide R2. One nit left standing: the board names the exception as `zi`/`cdi` and this clause names only `zi` — `cdi` is a bare alias to it, so the route is named, but `idioms` does not link `cdi` in `also`."}
    {id: "help", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "e80413a6a9359227", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r6", author: "impl-H-1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "rewritten twice this session. Round 1 restored the delegation step the old text skipped; round 2 restored the LAST step, which round 1 also dropped. All four steps of 06-help/02-help-command's Resolution order (prd.md:78-85) are now present and in order — no argument to the overview, a topic or entry name to the manual, a name the shell knows to std/help untouched, anything left to a manual search that falls through to std/help's own search — and `help --all` matches R5. Nothing is asserted that the PRD does not say. Spec-only, no live reading: `help` is this epic's own unimplemented subject. CAVEAT recorded: the collision case is unsettled — the same PRD's acceptance (prd.md:64-65) says `help ls` must reach the builtin while shell.nuon:111 documents `ls`, so `a name this manual does not document but the shell does` implicitly takes the manual-wins side. Logged as M-13 (prds/00-delivery/corrections/prd.md:220) and disclosed in this entry's own `why`."}
    {id: "help --check", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "47c6cb0c7f15c1f0", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "no live route; read against 06-help/04-drift-check R1-R3, R4, R7, R8 (prd.md:21-51) alone."}
    {id: "rr", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "32b814d8e9e5603b", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "alias confirmed config.nu:253, matching 04-shell/02-aliases-utilities R3. Qualifier: that PRD says nothing about idempotence; `applying twice changes nothing` is backed only by 05-platform/01-deploy-mechanism/prd.md:18."}
    {id: "pass <tab>", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "7dd4eeb31dac0bfe", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r1", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "theme", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "adb31fdbd5eb94d8", reviewer: "reader-S-9", author: "impl-S-9", date: "2026-08-22", note: "route walked end-to-end: bare `theme` falls through the dispatch (theme.nu:249-294) to `tv theme`; the channel source is `_theme_list` (cable/theme.toml.tmpl:27), which heads the list with the slot pair (theme.nu:209-228) and `no_sort`/`frecency = false` keep that head on top. Enter -> `_theme_commit` (theme.nu:239-247) applies and writes the id into the ACTIVE slot, as written; Esc -> `_theme_bg_restore` (theme.nu:186-189), which touches no theme state. Two qualifiers left standing: Esc's restore is an active OSC 11 re-assert of the current base00 (OSC 111 only when the yaml is unfindable), not a passive no-op; and under NO_COLOR the preview emits no OSC 11 at all (theme-preview.sh), so `retints live` assumes color is on. Matches spec04's mandated wording clause for clause."}
    {id: "theme toggle", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "435c6023cfc23d11", reviewer: "reader-S-9", author: "impl-S-9", date: "2026-08-22", note: "`theme toggle` -> `_theme_toggle` -> `_theme_use_slot` on the other slot (theme.nu:128-156): parked scheme applied, pointer moves — the route deliberately applies FIRST and moves the pointer after (the F6 double-press race), which the `use` glosses but does not contradict. `Run it again to come back` is spec01's own round-trip acceptance box. Naming F6 is mandated (spec04 and the PRD's acceptance require help to list the keybinding) and specified terminal-side at 02-terminal/01-appearance R11, confirmed live by spec01's reconciliation. QUALIFIER: this repo ships no wezterm config yet, so `which is bound to it` is true of the live terminal and the specified end state, not of anything home/ deploys today — the same present-tense-against-spec posture this record already accepts for `help --json` and the capsule rows."}
    {id: "theme slots", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "6c67a73e9ac63a12", reviewer: "reader-S-9", author: "impl-S-9", date: "2026-08-22", note: "the readout is literal: theme.nu:261-266 loops A then B printing the parked id with `*` only on the active slot; `theme a`/`theme b` dispatch to `_theme_use_slot`, which sets the slot active and applies its scheme — `activates that slot directly and applies its scheme` is exact. Qualifier left standing: the apply is skipped when that scheme is already showing (theme.nu:141), so re-activating the live slot fires no hook chain — an optimization, not a contradiction."}
    # nvim.nuon
    {id: "<leader>", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "f60a9eebb54c7f22", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r2", date: "2026-08-21", note: "the five groups and their names match which-key exactly (~/.config/nvim/lua/plugins/editor.lua:24-28). Qualifier: the leader menu also lists the bare leader maps (w, q, e, |, -, <space>), not only the groups."}
    {id: "<Esc>", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "a88215afd64071cf", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r5", author: "impl-H-1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "rewritten this session: the old text promised the search highlight would drop. The map is <cmd>nohlsearch<CR> (keymaps.lua:4) but `hlsearch` is off — options.lua:30, and mandated off by 03-editor/01-options R6 (prd.md:32-33) — so nothing is ever highlighted and the key produces nothing visible. The entry's own `why` conceded this in the past tense while the `use` still promised the result."}
    {id: "<C-h> <C-j> <C-k> <C-l>", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "cd431cad4bb453ce", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r2", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "<C-Up> <C-Down> <C-Left> <C-Right>", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "a8cbe39020e532f4", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r2", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "<leader>| and <leader>-", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "f9c7f6b4833dcc0f", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r2", date: "2026-08-21", note: "maps at keymaps.lua:16-17; the `right`/`below` claims hold only because splitright/splitbelow are set (options.lua:16-17), which the entry does not say and does not need to."}
    {id: "<S-h> <S-l> <leader>bd", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "15a352a9791c2b5e", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r5", author: "impl-H-1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "rewritten this session: `leaves the window where it was` is false. The map is a plain <cmd>bdelete<CR> (keymaps.lua:20), and plain :bdelete is exactly the command that disturbs the layout. Measured headless on this config with two windows and three listed buffers: before wins=2 bufs=3, after :bdelete wins=1 bufs=2 — the split closed. Layout preservation is what mini.bufremove / Snacks.bufdelete exist for; 03-editor/02-keymaps R3 claims nothing more than `delete`."}
    {id: "<A-j> <A-k>", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "143b606bfa795909", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r2", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "<C-d> <C-u> n N", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "d8a8b1bd50e81dd1", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r2", date: "2026-08-21", note: "maps are as described (keymaps.lua:28-31) and `zv` really does open the fold. Qualifier: scrolloff = 999 (options.lua:12) already pins the cursor line to the centre, so the re-centre the `use` advertises is not observable as a change — the zz is belt-and-braces."}
    {id: "< and > (visual)", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "6f6c4bc33710b216", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r2", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "<leader>w and <leader>q", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "4f658fed5c4e7473", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r2", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "<leader>p (visual)", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "bf216b5b81007ff0", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r2", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "<S-Up> <S-Down> <S-Left> <S-Right>", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "d7e518a56df21742", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r2", date: "2026-08-21", note: "the gesture matches live in all three modes (keymaps.lua:108-125), and insert <S-Left> really does catch two characters. The shift-select decision is settled (00-delivery/decisions/shift-select-scope/prd.md:28-34), so it excuses nothing. The qualifier is on the PRD side: 03-editor/14-shift-select/prd.md:54 says <S-Left> selects `the character just typed` (singular) while R5 (:36-38) mandates the correction for <S-Right> only. The `use` is honest about live; that acceptance line is what should be tightened."}
    {id: "h j k l (visual)", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "af64f590f82a205d", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r2", date: "2026-08-21", note: "keymaps.lua:127-129 maps h j k l and the four unshifted arrows, and visual_motion preserves vim.v.count in both branches (keymaps.lua:96-106) — the count claim and the `v-started selection extends` claim both hold."}
    {id: "<C-c> (visual)", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "1233c0e3c37a1160", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r2", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "<C-v> (visual)", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "d7c403aa39e10caa", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r2", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "<Tab> <S-Tab> <C-n> <C-p> <C-Space> <C-e>", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "fddbfb1a5838435c", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r2", date: "2026-08-21", note: "Tab/S-Tab/C-n/C-p, docs-after-200ms and signature help all match (plugins/completion.lua:13-25 against blink.cmp presets.lua:45-71). Qualifier: <C-Space> is { show, show_documentation, hide_documentation } — it opens the menu and then toggles the DOCUMENTATION, never hiding the menu, so `toggles the menu` is 03-editor/05-completion R2's word (prd.md:26) rather than the behaviour. <C-e> is cancel, not hide."}
    {id: "<leader>e", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "fc13dc24251d2444", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r2", date: "2026-08-21", note: "map at plugins/explorer.lua:10; oil.open(nil) resolves the parent URL of the current buffer (oil.nvim/lua/oil/init.lua:375-384) and show_hidden = true is set at explorer.lua:6. Adjacent, outside the `use`: this entry's `why` says `:e some/dir` reaches neither oil nor netrw, which is true of live (netrwPlugin disabled at config/lazy.lua:34) but denies 03-editor/06-explorer acceptance criterion 3 (prd.md:30) — a correction against that PRD, not against this entry."}
    {id: "<leader>cf", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "4846d48cb5e40e26", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r2", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "<leader>t", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "8afaa8acc59bdd7e", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r5", author: "impl-H-1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "rewritten this session: `move between cells` under the <leader>t prefix is false. The prefix maps are built from g:table_mode_map_prefix (vim-table-mode/plugin/table-mode.vim:31, :48-57, :74) but the cell motions are hard-wired to {| }| [| ]| at :40-43 and never consult it, so a reader hunting for cell movement under <leader>t finds nothing. 03-editor/15-markdown-tables R3 (prd.md:24-26) specifies only the prefix."}
    {id: "<leader>ff and <leader><space>", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "0c2ae661e4bc591e", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r2", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "<leader>fg", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "bc08cf0b142c3f1c", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r2", date: "2026-08-21", note: "map at plugins/telescope.lua:14; <CR> with nothing marked falls through to actions.select_default (telescope.lua:31), which opens the file on the match line."}
    {id: "<leader>fb", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "9ed6d3ba7ece9d61", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r2", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "<leader>fh", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "e28450499cb97ed6", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r2", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "<Tab> <S-Tab> <CR> (telescope)", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "2691b51953055d73", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r5", author: "impl-H-1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "rewritten this session: the directions were inverted. Live binds toggle_selection + move_selection_worse / + move_selection_better (plugins/telescope.lua:39-40). sorting_strategy is unset, so telescope's default `descending` applies (telescope.nvim/lua/telescope/config.lua:144-145); Picker:get_row = max_results - index (pickers.lua:376-382) puts the BEST match on the BOTTOM row, and Picker:move_selection adds the delta to the ROW (pickers.lua:909-911) with better = +1 / worse = -1 (pickers/scroller.lua:112-121). So <Tab> (worse) moves UP the screen. 03-editor/08-telescope R4 (prd.md:29-32) deliberately says worse/better; the entry mistranslated it."}
    {id: "gd and gI", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "d0e0edc7a74aa65b", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r2", date: "2026-08-21", note: "maps at plugins/lsp.lua:30-31, buffer-local on LspAttach (lsp.lua:24). Adjacent, outside the `use`: the verify target omits `desc`, which per the file header means compare against the title, but live sets `LSP: Goto definition` / `LSP: Goto implementation` (lsp.lua:28-31) — a drift-check false failure waiting for 04-drift-check, not a `use` defect."}
    {id: "<leader>rn and <leader>ca", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "c0df9bb17a7735fc", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r2", date: "2026-08-21", note: "maps at plugins/lsp.lua:32-33. Same absent-`desc` mismatch as `gd and gI`: live descs are `LSP: Rename` / `LSP: Code action`."}
    {id: "Neovim's own LSP keys", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "23e653c26ae6a046", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r5", author: "impl-H-1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "rewritten this session: `]d and [d ... in a bordered float` is false on this Neovim. Live is 0.12.4, where ]d/[d are vim.diagnostic.jump({count=...}) with no float (runtime/lua/vim/_core/defaults.lua:263-269); since 0.12 the float on jump is opt-in via jump.on_jump and this config never sets it. The key that opens the bordered float is <C-W>d (_core/defaults.lua:280-282), which the entry never named — a whole missing step. The inline dot and the bordered/source float ARE configured (plugins/lsp.lua:15-19), which is why the claim looked right."}
    # terminal.nuon
    {id: "F5 <digit>", file: "terminal.nuon", digest: "8cf28d438874862a", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r3", date: "2026-08-21", note: "F5 pushes jump_mode one_shot/until_unknown (wezterm.lua:1003-1017), digits map to ActivateTab (:956-966), Escape only unpaints (:951-954). Qualifier: the mode also ends on timeout_milliseconds = JUMP_TIMEOUT_MS = 5000 (wezterm.lua:702) — wait six seconds and the digit you finally press types into the pane instead. `over the moment you press the second key` is true but not the whole exit story."}
    {id: "Ctrl+Shift+<arrow>", file: "terminal.nuon", digest: "695513b585681e4e", reviewer: "implementer-f5-jump-r1", author: "implementer-f5-jump", date: "2026-08-22", note: "read against 02-terminal/03-f5-jump-mode R4 (prd.md:61-66) and the SOURCE TREE, the rebuilt config being undeployed: show-keys --lua over home/dot_config/wezterm/wezterm.lua reports all four SHIFT|CTRL arrow rows -> ActivatePaneDirection (dump lines 136-142), and nothing shadows them — config.keys holds only F6, Ctrl+Shift+Q and F5 (wezterm.lua:648-719), key_tables only jump_mode (:372), no disable_default_key_bindings anywhere. `over a running TUI included` holds because the assignment resolves in the GUI key pass before the pty sees the chord, and it survives an open jump_mode, whose until_unknown pops without eating so the chord still lands (wezterm.lua:706-716). Verify mods spelled SHIFT|CTRL exactly as show-keys prints arrows, per the header's folding rule (terminal.nuon:24-29). Qualifier left standing: `focus moves to the pane in that direction` presumes a split — on a single-pane tab the chord is a silent no-op, and the floor spawns single-pane tabs; the title's `between panes` carries that."}
    {id: "Ctrl+Shift+Q", file: "terminal.nuon", digest: "aa0f069fcc1f962c", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r3", date: "2026-08-21", note: "live is mark_closing then one CloseCurrentTab{confirm=false} per tab (wezterm.lua:1058-1074) and show-keys reports { key = 'Q', mods = 'CTRL' }, so `without asking` is right. Finding for corrections: 02-terminal/02-startup-layout R3 (prd.md:24) requires WezTerm's confirmation for running processes; the live config disables it deliberately because the prompt would fire once per tab. The entry follows live, per the file header; the PRD is what needs correcting."}
    {id: "nine tabs", file: "terminal.nuon", digest: "e6c556069b82c868", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r3", date: "2026-08-21", note: "startup is as described (wezterm.lua:296-297, TAB_COUNT = 9 at :41). Qualifier: full-screen is true of the STARTUP window only — toggle_fullscreen() is called nowhere else, so a window spawned later is padded to nine tabs on window-focus-changed (:302-303) but never fullscreened. Also 02-terminal/02-startup-layout/prd.md:20,26 says maximized where live fullscreens; live wins and the PRD needs the correction."}
    {id: "lit and dim tabs", file: "terminal.nuon", digest: "5de7908e99fe928a", reviewer: "implementer-tab-content-state-r1", author: "implementer-tab-content-state", date: "2026-08-23", note: "new entry, read against the SOURCE TREE and not against a live GUI — occupancy is a live-session property, so the visible half of this behaviour is the six T.6 rows in gates/manual/wave4.md and none of it was watched happening. Every claim traced. `the dim inactive colour` is colors.tab_bar.inactive_tab, bg_color = theme.background and fg_color = base03 (wezterm.lua:668-671), which the handler leaves in charge by returning a bare string. `the same digit lit` is the one other return, wezterm.format with Foreground AnsiColor Silver over the same label (:870-873) — an ANSI slot name, so no colour value is asserted here. `within five seconds` is config.status_update_interval = 5000 (:992) reaching learn_pane_programs, registered on update-status, window-config-reloaded, pane-focus-changed and window-focus-changed (:815-818); the update-status tick is the only one of the four this build actually fires periodically, which is why the sentence says five seconds rather than immediately. `with nothing to refresh` follows from the same tick being periodic rather than keystroke-driven. `anywhere inside it` is tab_is_occupied looping over tab.panes and returning true from inside the loop (:830-843), the OR over panes. `a focused tab looks the same whether it is busy or not` is the tab.is_active early return (:863-865), which hands back the bare label before the tint is ever reached. `Nothing to press` is right: this node binds no key, which is also why verify is prose — a tab colour has no show-keys handle."}
    {id: "Ctrl+Shift+X", file: "terminal.nuon", digest: "8ba99173909b34da", reviewer: "implementer-copy-mode-r1", author: "implementer-copy-mode", date: "2026-08-22", note: "re-read this session because the `use` gained its search pointer. Every claim re-derived from the source tree and a fresh `wezterm show-keys --lua` dump rather than trusted from the earlier note: 54 builtin motions, because the effective copy_mode table prints 55 rows and exactly one of them is this config's `c` (wezterm.lua:419-420); the two-press cycle is SetSelectionMode Cell then Multiple of CopyTo ClipboardAndPrimarySelection and CopyMode Close (wezterm.lua:425-437); `y` really does copy AND close in one key, its row being act.Multiple of CopyTo ClipboardAndPrimarySelection and CopyMode Close; `q` and `Esc` are both act.CopyMode Close, so `leave with nothing copied` is exact. The new tail is measured, not asserted: the copy_mode block has zero hits for Search, NextMatch, PriorMatch, ClearPattern and CycleMatchType, and the only act.Search row in the whole dump is the top-level 'F' with 'CTRL' default — so `no search of any kind` and `searching is Ctrl+Shift+F` are both true against the binary, and both are R3 of the source PRD."}
    {id: "Ctrl+V", file: "terminal.nuon", digest: "b43982c6b68c5174", reviewer: "implementer-copy-mode-r1", author: "implementer-copy-mode", date: "2026-08-22", note: "the `use` is byte-identical to the reviewed text; only `source` moved, from the epic to 02-terminal/04-copy-mode, and that move is what closes the qualifier the previous reading recorded. R6 now specifies this key: PasteFrom Clipboard, a bracketed paste, no subprocess, reaching both a shell and a running program (Claude, nvim). Live route re-read: wezterm.lua:813 is the single `v` with CTRL entry and it carries no callback, and the dump holds exactly one act.PasteFrom Clipboard row for `v` with CTRL. One qualifier left standing: `no reindenting in the editor` is a consequence of bracketed paste rather than a sentence R6 writes — R6 names the mechanism and this is what the mechanism buys."}
    {id: "Ctrl+C", file: "terminal.nuon", digest: "a0c8f17d061af8a9", reviewer: "implementer-copy-mode-r1", author: "implementer-copy-mode", date: "2026-08-22", note: "the `use` is byte-identical to the reviewed text; only `source` moved to 02-terminal/04-copy-mode, and that closes the gap the previous reading recorded, which was that the only Ctrl+C text in the subtree described a different, non-existent behaviour. R7 now specifies exactly what the entry says: a non-empty selection copies to ClipboardAndPrimarySelection and then clears, otherwise SendKey c with CTRL, and `never both` is the requirement's own wording. Live route: wezterm.lua:822-828, the selection read first at :822, the clear at :825, the fallthrough at :827 — the order the `use` describes. The dump carries exactly one callback row for `c` with CTRL."}
    {id: "Ctrl+Shift+D", file: "terminal.nuon", digest: "ab6a1b072fab3a1c", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r3", date: "2026-08-21", note: "unbuilt — no D/CTRL anywhere in show-keys — so read against 01-capsule/01-container-lifecycle alone: /workspace and initial cwd R5 (:38), credentials :39, thin wrapper Purpose (:19-21), reuse R2 (:27). Two qualifiers: R3 (:30) allows an implicit image build on the first press, which is not immediate and the `use` gives no hint of a wait; and R2's second sentence (:28) covers the stopped case, which is start-then-attach rather than `back in the same running container`."}
    {id: "Ctrl+Shift+B", file: "terminal.nuon", digest: "475abe2045e9f160", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r3", date: "2026-08-21", note: "against 01-capsule/01-container-lifecycle R4 (:33-35) the wording is exact. Finding the file header does NOT record: Ctrl+Shift+B IS bound live, to the wallpaper PromptInputLine (wezterm.lua:1094-1096), so its verify target {key: B, mods: CTRL} false-passes today off a binding that does something else — the same problem the header records for Ctrl+Shift+T only."}
    {id: "Ctrl+Shift+S", file: "terminal.nuon", digest: "486a386f67b588c3", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r5", author: "impl-H-1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "rewritten this session: unbuilt, so read against 01-capsule/04-recent-workspaces alone. R2 (prd.md:24-26) specifies a FUZZY-SELECTABLE list and the old `use` said only `pick from the directories` — it named no key, no typing, no confirm, so the whole middle of the gesture was missing against the `what to press next` contract. The replacement adds only what R2 states; it deliberately does not invent a confirm key, because the PRD names none."}
    {id: "Ctrl+Shift+T", file: "terminal.nuon", digest: "08407945936f0904", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r3", date: "2026-08-21", note: "the delta is exactly R2's second clause (01-capsule/04-recent-workspaces/prd.md:32-33). It delegates to Ctrl+Shift+S (`same picker`), so it inherits that entry's gesture; fixing that one fixes this. The key really is taken — show-keys reports { key = 'T', mods = 'CTRL', action = SpawnTab } and the tab reconciler treats that default as the manual new-tab path (wezterm.lua:189-191) — which the entry's own `why` states honestly and defers to the terminal re-spec."}
    {id: "F6", file: "terminal.nuon", digest: "d1ff23fcdc9be236", reviewer: "implementer-terminal-appearance-r1", author: "implementer-terminal-appearance", date: "2026-08-22", note: "read against the SOURCE TREE (the rebuilt config is unbuilt live), not ~/.config. Every claim traced: `the scheme parked in the other slot` is _theme_toggle = _theme_use_slot(_theme_other_slot(...)) applying via ^tinty apply (theme.nu:153-156, :141); `press it again to come back` holds because slots are a two-element A/B flip and the apply-before-pointer ordering (theme.nu:132-142) keeps a rapid second press returning; `every window, tab and pane at once` is colors.lua on the reload watch (wezterm.lua:54) -> window-config-reloaded -> retint_all_panes injecting OSC into every pane of every tab of every window (wezterm.lua:208-218), deduped in GLOBAL; `even over a full-screen TUI` because the binding is WezTerm-level (wezterm.lua:310-311) and the injection is OSC-only, no printable output."}
    {id: "Ctrl+Shift+F", file: "terminal.nuon", digest: "81c96a44695e0ed2", reviewer: "implementer-copy-mode-r1", author: "implementer-copy-mode", date: "2026-08-22", note: "new entry, and this reading corrected it. R3 of the source PRD says search lives in a separate 10-row search_mode table reached from normal mode by WezTerm's default Ctrl+Shift+F, which the config neither sets nor shadows. Verified in the dump: search_mode holds exactly 10 rows, and the top-level 'F' with 'CTRL' act.Search row is present and unshadowed, nothing in wezterm.lua touching either. The correction: the first draft said Enter steps through the matches, and the table actually maps Enter to CopyMode PriorMatch — upwards, not forwards. The shipped text names Enter and Ctrl+P as up, Ctrl+N as down, Ctrl+R as CycleMatchType and Escape as Close, each read off its own row."}
    {id: "copymode", file: "terminal.nuon", digest: "ce5f300b3cea35fa", reviewer: "implementer-copy-mode-r1", author: "implementer-copy-mode", date: "2026-08-22", note: "new entry, read against R5 and against both halves of the live route. copymode.nu prints one OSC 1337 SetUserVar named copymode, and the bytes were compared rather than eyeballed: nu sourcing the module and running the command gives exactly the 32 bytes ESC ]1337;SetUserVar=copymode=MQ== BEL, cmp-equal to the expected string. wezterm.lua:398-402 is the handler; it matches that one name and calls enter_copy_mode(window, pane), the same function the Ctrl+Shift+X binding calls (wezterm.lua:805-808), which is what makes `exactly as Ctrl+Shift+X leaves it` literally true rather than a resemblance. `the pane it runs in` is the handler's own pane argument, the pane the escape came off. mode shell is right, and the file header now records why: this one is typed, not pressed."}
    {id: "Ctrl+Alt+Super+drag", file: "terminal.nuon", digest: "52623694df2bd829", reviewer: "implementer-copy-mode-r1", author: "implementer-copy-mode", date: "2026-08-22", note: "new entry, read against R8. Live route: wezterm.lua:840-845, a Down event on the Left button with streak 1 and mods CTRL, ALT and SUPER, action act.StartWindowDrag; SUPER is Cmd, which is how the `use` spells it for a reader. `An ordinary drag still selects text` holds because config.mouse_bindings extends WezTerm's defaults rather than replacing them — disable_default_mouse_bindings is set nowhere — and neither of the two entries claims a bare left button. Verify is prose for a measured reason: wezterm show-keys --lua prints no mouse bindings at all on this build, zero hits for the word mouse in the whole dump, so there is no introspectable handle to name."}
    {id: "Ctrl+click", file: "terminal.nuon", digest: "60eac29cc453c9a1", reviewer: "implementer-copy-mode-r1", author: "implementer-copy-mode", date: "2026-08-22", note: "new entry, read against R8. Live route: wezterm.lua:851-857, an Up event on the Left button with mods CTRL, mouse_reporting true, action act.OpenLinkAtMouseCursor — Up for the click and Down for the drag, so the two bindings cannot collide on the same gesture. `even while nvim or another full-screen TUI owns the mouse` is exactly what the mouse_reporting flag buys under DECSET 1002/1006; without it WezTerm forwards the click to the application, which R8 states and the binding's comment carries. `A plain click still reaches the program` holds for the same reason as the drag entry: the defaults are extended, not replaced, and no bare left button is bound here. Verify is prose because the dump prints no mouse bindings to check against."}
    # capsule.nuon
    {id: "capsule [dir]", file: "capsule.nuon", digest: "d8b5a9e121cf562a", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r5", author: "impl-H-1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "PRD-only, no live reading: `which capsule` finds nothing and no Dockerfile or CLI is in the tree. Rewritten this session, three findings against 01-capsule/01-container-lifecycle. (1) The old text put the stopped-start AND a full image rebuild inside a sentence whose only stated timing was `well under a second`; R2 (:26-29) scopes that target to the RUNNING case, and the epic says cold start is dominated by docker itself (01-capsule/prd.md:29-30). (2) `a shell` under-described what comes back: the acceptance line says zsh (:43-45) and 02-dev-image R1 (:20-22) makes zsh+oh-my-zsh the interactive shell — on a surface whose whole point is that the container shell is not the host's nushell. (3) R7 (:39-40) makes every successful mount record the directory for the recent-workspaces picker, a user-visible side effect the entry never mentioned."}
    {id: "capsule --rebuild", file: "capsule.nuon", digest: "d83a529edbba865e", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r4", date: "2026-08-21", note: "PRD-only, no live reading — capsule is absent from PATH. The core claim is 01-capsule/01-container-lifecycle R4 in substance (:30-32). Two qualifiers left standing: `anything you left outside /workspace is gone` is PRD-silent, a sound inference from `recreates the container` plus R5's mount (:33-35) but stated by no requirement; and the `use` never says what comes back after the rebuild, because R4 does not either — a PRD gap, not an entry defect."}
    {id: "capsule list", file: "capsule.nuon", digest: "dc82e5e320465022", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r4", date: "2026-08-21", note: "PRD-only, no live reading. R6 (01-capsule/01-container-lifecycle/prd.md:36-38) says only `a subcommand to list capsules and remove stopped/all ones`. Two things stand on nothing written: the subcommand NAME `capsule list` is the manual's invention, and the output shape (`whether each is running or stopped`) is specified nowhere. Neither contradicts the PRD, so the entry stands — but R6 needs tightening before this entry can ever be met against a real surface."}
    {id: "capsule clean", file: "capsule.nuon", digest: "0a24be57a0158a80", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r5", author: "impl-H-1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "PRD-only, no live reading. Rewritten this session: the old text asserted a default the PRD does not specify (`removes THE STOPPED capsules`) and gave no gesture at all for the second mode (`when you ask it to`), which is unactionable and breaks the `what to press next` contract head-on. R6 (01-capsule/01-container-lifecycle/prd.md:36-38) lists `stopped/all` side by side and names neither the subcommand nor a flag. CORRECTIONS FILING NEEDED against R6: until it names the invocation, no `use` here can give the gesture, and inventing `--all` would be the same false record pointing the other way. The replacement says what is specified and says plainly that the rest is not settled."}
    {id: "credentials in a capsule", file: "capsule.nuon", digest: "d922b7e911810476", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r4", date: "2026-08-21", note: "PRD-only, no live reading. 01-capsule/03-credential-propagation/prd.md:35-39 is near clause-for-clause: `git pull`/`git push` over both SSH and HTTPS and `ssh -T` with no prompt (:36-38), claude and opencode start authenticated (:39). `Nothing to run` is the right opener for a prose-verified concept entry, and the mechanism sits in `why` rather than `use`, which is the field split the README asks for."}
]
